# AURA 2.0 Architecture

## Overview

AURA 2.0 is designed as a modular AI assistant platform with a clear split between a frontend interface and a backend API service layer. The system is built to support chat, data analysis, image ideas, video concepts, offline-readiness, and personal assistant workflows.

## Core goals

- Provide a general-purpose AI chat experience
- Add data analysis and summarization tools
- Support future image and video generation integrations
- Keep the UI lightweight and developer friendly
- Make the backend provider-agnostic and extensible
- Support future offline persistence and location-aware features

## High-level architecture

```text
Frontend (React + Vite)
        |
        |  HTTP requests
        v
Backend (Express + TypeScript)
        |
        |  normalized service calls
        v
AI Provider Layer
  - OpenAI compatible endpoints
  - future: Gemini, Anthropic, Azure, ElevenLabs
        |
        v
Response + orchestration
```

## Layers

### 1. Frontend layer

The frontend is responsible for:
- showing the AI interface
- collecting user prompts
- rendering assistant responses
- triggering feature flows such as chat, creative ideation, and analysis

### 2. API layer

The backend exposes endpoints for:
- chat conversations
- AI feature discovery
- image prompt generation
- video concept generation
- analysis and summarization

This layer validates inputs and centralizes business logic.

### 3. AI service layer

AI logic is isolated in a service module. This keeps the code maintainable and allows easy switching between providers.

The current starter includes:
- OpenAI integration via the official SDK
- fallback response behavior when no API key is configured
- structured response objects for image and video workflows

### 4. Extensibility layer

This project is intentionally structured to support future expansion with:
- user profiles and sessions
- external data connectors
- location-aware workflows
- vector memory or RAG
- offline caching and sync
- voice or speech interfaces

## Request flow

1. User enters a prompt in the React UI.
2. Frontend sends a POST request to the backend API.
3. Backend validates payload and routes it to the AI service.
4. AI service calls a configured provider or returns fallback text.
5. Response is serialized and displayed in the UI.

## Feature groups

### Conversational assistant

- chat prompts
- daily tasks
- planning recommendations
- summarization

### Data analysis

- parsing user text into insights
- summarizing trends or themes
- structured analysis for future dashboards

### Creative generation

- text-to-image prompts
- concept generation for short-form video
- product storytelling and narrative scripts

### Personal assistant workflows

- local-first reminders
- profile-aware recommendations
- future memory and personalization support

## Deployment direction

The repo is ready for local development and containerized deployment. A future production progression could include:

- PostgreSQL for user data and sessions
- Redis for caching and queueing
- cloud storage for generated assets
- auth and role-based access control
- load-balanced API deployments

## Recommended next milestones

1. Add authentication and user profiles
2. Add persistent memory or database-backed session history
3. Connect a real image generation provider
4. Connect a real video generation workflow
5. Add location-aware features and offline sync
6. Package the backend and frontend into production deployments

This architecture is intentionally simple but scalable enough to grow into a genuine AI personal assistant platform.
